# {{ project_name }} agent instructions

## Responsibility map

- GitHub Issues and pull requests define work, progress, and evidence. Approved specs and ADRs preserve durable context; add a plan only for cross-session, high-risk, or hard-to-recover work, and never store raw chat transcripts. Follow [Journey 01](docs/index.html#method) for work-item relationships and fields.
- `AGENTS.md` is the single source for AI working instructions; `CLAUDE.md` only imports it.
- One writable task uses one Git branch and one worktree so concurrent changes stay isolated.
- Reusable validation lives in `scripts/` and tests. Each work item's release level sets a minimum cumulative suite: alpha→baseline, beta→fast, early→docs, formal→full; changed-path risk may only raise that floor. `CSARC_VERIFICATION_SUITE=<baseline|fast|docs> ./scripts/verify-fast` and `./scripts/verify` (full) run locally, while hosted `verify` only validates their fresh `Verified-locally:` trailer. Running the resolved suite before every push is mandatory. The exact hosted-bot allowlist (currently only `dependabot[bot]`) runs that suite on its own repository branch; it does not relax review or other required checks. Checked-in files under `.github/workflows/` are the only active GitHub Actions. See [CI/CD settings](docs/index.html#testing) and [`docs/ci-policy.md`](docs/ci-policy.md) for resolution and stage ownership.
- [Journey 08](docs/index.html#governance) and the [`docs/ci-policy.md` quota fallback](docs/ci-policy.md#failure-與-fallback) own review requirements, merge eligibility, Alpha self-merge, and quota fallback. Do not restate or invent exceptions here.
- Copier owns template updates. Follow [Journey 09](docs/index.html#template-release) for the update contract.

## Scope and sources of truth

- This file applies repository-wide. A nearer `AGENTS.md` may add subtree-specific guidance.
- `.csarc/config.yml` is the template-managed source for enabled language and repository modules.
- Use the linked Issue or `docs/specs/` SDD contract for scope; `docs/README.md` maps durable project memory.
- Use `docs/adr/` for decisions. On updated projects, also search project-owned `docs/decisions/`; never move or overwrite it automatically.
- Edit project site content in `site/content/_index.zh-tw.md` / `_index.en.md` and theme overrides in `docs/site-theme.css`, then rebuild with `./scripts/build-repo-site`; do not hand-edit the generated `docs/index.html` / `docs/index.en.html`.

## Working loop

1. At task start, run only `git worktree list --porcelain`; reserve unscoped cleanup for explicit maintenance. Creator cleanup after merge remains required. Before planning or opening an Issue or Milestone, list open Milestones and open Issues, then search open and closed Issues with two to four concrete problem or technology terms. Inspect the body, comments, and linked pull requests of credible matches; titles and labels alone are not decisions. Keep each query bounded to 20 candidates.
2. Use the work-item model in [Journey 01](docs/index.html#method). A Milestone is an optional dated delivery or release bucket; follow `docs/milestone-description.md`. Projects stay disabled.
3. Before creating the work item, present a short prior-decision summary when the user can respond. If creation is already authorized, record each relevant `#N` as preserved, superseded, or rejected with a reason in the Issue supplement or Milestone `References`; when none are found, record the bounded queries used. When a user confirms a durable architecture, tooling, security, compatibility, or platform constraint, summarize it in the work item and update the matching `docs/adr/` record through the scoped pull request. Never store a raw conversation transcript or promote an unconfirmed inference to a decision. Never silently reverse an earlier decision.
4. Use the checked-in Issue form and pull request template whether creating through the UI, CLI, or API; do not create a second body format.
5. Open or select one GitHub Issue before editing and use its acceptance criteria as scope. Select its release level in the Issue form; a Milestone work Issue inherits the tracker level and must not conflict with it. Only declarations from repository collaborators are trusted; otherwise governance uses the configured default (alpha for a newly generated repository). State the locally required suite (release-level floor plus path risk) and what hosted CI proves. Titles use 12-80 ASCII characters, at least three words, no type prefix, and no trailing period. Set one native Type, matching label, assignee, and applicable parent and Milestone. Use `scripts/gh-issue-edit` instead of raw `gh issue edit` for body changes so an existing approval cannot be invalidated without a local warning; the wrapper is advisory and cannot intercept GitHub UI or direct API edits. The creator is the default owner: UI creators assign themselves, while CLI and agents pass `--assignee @me`; change the assignee only for an explicit handoff.
6. If new work exceeds that Issue, stop and open a separate Issue; do not silently widen the current branch or pull request. When that new Issue surfaces from a problem discovered while working a Milestone Issue, default to keeping it inside that same Milestone (Milestone-tagged, based on its `dev/m<N>` delivery branch) rather than opening it standalone. Open it standalone only when it clearly meets one of: it spans multiple Milestones or is a governance/tooling mechanism affecting all future Milestones, not this Milestone's own feature scope; it is an urgent production fix that cannot wait for the Milestone to close; or it originates from an external report (another collaborator or peer session) with no direct causal link to this Milestone's own work. When unsure which applies, default to keeping it in the Milestone — the exceptions are narrow, not a default escape hatch.
7. Check every acceptance-criteria box in the Issue, and every pull request checklist box, only once it carries real evidence, and finish all of them before opening a pull request or pushing a commit meant to satisfy the closing keyword — not incrementally, box by box, across separate pushes. Each push re-triggers the same PR policy gate; pushing a commit you already know is incomplete guarantees that gate fails. Keep the pull request in draft while work is still incomplete: the acceptance-checklist gate does not enforce completeness on a draft, and revalidates in full the moment it is marked ready for review — see `docs/ci-policy.md`'s "Acceptance-checklist 驗證時機（#573）" section.
8. {% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}For a Milestone Issue, start from its one short-lived `dev/m<milestone>-short-slug` delivery branch. For an Issue without a Milestone, start from current `main`; if independent changes must soak or ship together, create a real Milestone instead of a catch-all branch.{% else %}Start from `{{ branch_strategy }}` or an open work branch whose pull request chain ends there.{% endif %} Use `gh issue develop <issue> --base <base> --name type/<issue-number>-short-slug` for the native Development link, and follow `docs/ci-policy.md` for explicitly authorized canary, synchronization, promotion, hotfix, and recovery routes. When a pull request is stacked on another work branch, retarget it to the shared integration branch as soon as the base pull request is ready to merge, before that branch is squash-merged and deleted. GitHub auto-closes (does not auto-retarget) a pull request whose base branch no longer exists; recovering it means opening a new pull request from the same head branch, which loses the original PR's number, reviews, and comment history.
9. Inspect nearby code, tests, and configuration before choosing an implementation.
10. Make the smallest coherent change; preserve unrelated work and project-owned source files.
11. Add or update the narrowest regression test that proves non-trivial behavior.
12. Delegates run scoped or focused checks while iterating. Resolve the release level, map it to baseline／fast／docs／full, then raise that floor when `scripts/ci_tier.py` finds riskier changed paths (`docs/ci-policy.md`). Before every push, run `CSARC_VERIFICATION_SUITE=<resolved-suite> ./scripts/verify-fast`; it delegates to `./scripts/verify` when the result is full and writes the exact attestation hosted CI validates. A later base-only re-merge creates a new unattested commit, so it must rerun the resolved suite too.
13. Before any automated PR Ready/Draft, authorization, metadata, or merge write, acquire the remote lease and use `scripts/pr_lifecycle.py`; otherwise remain read-only. Follow Journey 08 rather than duplicating merge rules here.
14. After merge, leave the task worktree and run `./scripts/cleanup-worktrees --apply --worktree <path> origin/<pull-request-base>` from another checkout. Never remove another task's worktree or any dirty, locked, detached, unmerged, or unverifiable worktree.
15. Report the changed behavior, verification evidence, and any remaining limitation.

Writable agents use one branch and worktree per independent task. Reuse a host-managed worktree; never force a branch into multiple worktrees or remove one owned by another task or with uncommitted changes. Use local storage for active checkouts, worktrees, environments, and caches. If assigned a cloud-synced File Provider path, switch to a local checkout without routine user confirmation; preserve existing changes. Worktrees isolate files; integration still uses PR, CI, review, and final verification.

Never push directly to an integration branch, invent successful evidence, create or dispatch release tags manually, or bypass the linked governance policy.

Duplicate triage may close an Issue without code changes when it links the canonical Issue and uses GitHub's native duplicate reason. If a change or `copier update` modifies an Issue or pull request body shape, migrate existing bodies before closing it; otherwise create and link a follow-up Issue first.

Existing-repository history changes start read-only: propose semantic story groups and exclusions, wait for explicit confirmation, then update Milestones and associations idempotently. Never infer groups from titles or labels alone, require every historical Issue to have a Milestone, or reopen completed Issues.

## Commands

{% if "python" in languages %}- Python setup: `uv sync --locked`.
{% endif %}{% if "typescript" in languages %}- TypeScript setup: `corepack enable && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
{% endif %}{% if "rust" in languages %}- Rust setup: `rustup show && cargo fetch --locked`. On Linux/WSL2, compiling this project also needs a system C linker -- install `build-essential` (or the equivalent toolchain) first; a machine with only `rustup` fails with `error: linker 'cc' not found`.
{% endif %}{% if not languages %}- Environment setup: no language toolchain install is required.
{% endif %}- Site source check: `./scripts/build-repo-site --check`.
{% if "python" in languages %}- Python iteration: `uv run ruff check <paths>`, `uv run ty check`, and `uv run pytest <test-path>`.
{% endif %}{% if "typescript" in languages %}- TypeScript iteration: `pnpm exec biome check <paths>`, `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit`, and `pnpm exec vitest run <test-path>`.
{% endif %}{% if "rust" in languages %}- Rust iteration: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy --locked --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, and `cargo test --locked`.
{% endif %}- Daily PR gate: `CSARC_VERIFICATION_SUITE=<baseline|fast|docs|full> ./scripts/verify-fast`, mandatory before every push; it writes the exact-suite `Verified-locally:` trailer hosted CI validates.
- Full delivery verification: the same command delegates to `./scripts/verify` when the resolved suite is full; `./scripts/verify <mode>` remains focused evidence and does not attest.
- Self-check an attestation before pushing: `./scripts/check-verify-attestation HEAD` (add `--required-tier full` to match what a full-tier PR needs).
{% if pr_review_mode | default('human') == 'copilot' %}- Copilot review loop (`pr_review_mode: copilot` in `.csarc/config.yml`): after each push, `python3 scripts/review_gate.py status --repo <owner/repo> --pr <N>` lists Copilot's findings on the current head and the unresolved threads; fix them locally, push, answer and resolve the threads, and repeat until Copilot's review of the exact head is clean and the `review` check passes, then merge through `scripts/pr_lifecycle.py`. A maintainer approval of the exact head remains a valid alternative. See `docs/ci-policy.md`'s "Copilot 審核模式（#752）" section.
{% endif %}- Release execution (if this repository owns `release.yml`): hosted Automatic/Guided publish is a known permanent limitation, not a bug to chase — `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot prove the Immutable Releases setting and this fails closed by design. Run `scripts/publish-release` locally under the maintainer's own admin identity instead; see `docs/ci-policy.md`'s hosted-release-limitation section.
- Milestone preflight: `python3 scripts/sync_milestone_state.py preflight --repo <repo> --milestone <N>` validates a Milestone's due date, tracker title, and `Lifecycle Issue: #N` link before dispatching work under it, instead of waiting for the first PR to fail `Validate Milestone approval` — see `docs/milestone-description.md`.

## Editing boundaries

{% if not languages %}- This profile does not create a product language module. New product code requires an intentional profile change before language-specific CI is claimed.
{% else %}- Product code under {% if "python" in languages %}`src/{{ package_name }}/` and `tests/`{% endif %}{% if "typescript" in languages %}{% if "python" in languages %}, plus {% endif %}`typescript/src/` and `typescript/tests/`{% endif %}{% if "rust" in languages %}{% if "python" in languages or "typescript" in languages %}, plus {% endif %}`src/lib.rs`{% endif %} belongs to this project. Copier updates must not overwrite it.
- Do not hand-edit generated lockfiles. Use the selected language package manager so integrity metadata stays valid.
{% endif %}
- Keep third-party GitHub Actions pinned to full commit SHAs.
- Do not create a separate GitHub repository for testing or validation. Use local temporary projects or this repository's Issues, branches, pull requests, and Actions.
- Comments in source code must be written in English. Leave style details in the selected language's executable configuration instead of copying them here.

## Platform capability and automation

- Keep desired automation declarative and testable; do not assume organization or enterprise administrator access.
- Never extract or persist `gh auth token` as project configuration, require a personal token for the portable baseline, or treat a permission error as proof that a capability is available.
- Report blocked or unavailable capability explicitly. Never claim a release, deployment, review gate, or other control succeeded when it was skipped or unsupported.

## Safety

- Never commit secrets, tokens, private keys, or populated `.env` files. Use GitHub Secrets or approved secret management for automation.
- Review `scripts/apply-repository-settings.sh plan` before `apply`; do not operate production or external infrastructure without explicit authorization.
- Do not bypass failed CI, required checks, review, CODEOWNER approval, or supply-chain verification. Journey 08 is the only source for any merge exception or Alpha self-merge authorization.

## Code Review Rules

- Verify the change satisfies the Issue acceptance criteria and has relevant tests.
- Flag unintended public API or release behavior changes; require an explicit compatibility and SemVer decision.
- Review dependency, workflow-permission, secret-handling, and artifact-verification changes as security-sensitive.
- Prefer existing project patterns and focused changes over new abstractions or broad rewrites.

## Nested instructions

- Add a nested `AGENTS.md` only when a subtree has genuinely different commands, ownership, or safety boundaries.
- Use `AGENTS.override.md` only when replacing same-directory guidance is intentional; do not pre-create empty instruction files.
